WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.210
Speaker 1: It's great to welcome to the program
today, Tess Wilkinson.

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:08.590
Ryan, who's a moral psychologist and a law
professor at the University of Pennsylvania

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:14.099
and also author of the recently released book
Full Proof How Fear of Playing the Sucker

00:00:14.099 --> 00:00:18.310
Shapes Ourselves and the Social Order and
What We Can Do About It.

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:19.430
Really great having you on today.

00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:20.430
I appreciate your time.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:22.029
Speaker 5: Thank you so much for having me.

00:00:22.029 --> 00:00:29.420
Speaker 1: So to start with, let's talk maybe
about what is the sucker construct or that

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:32.340
idea that you talk about and elaborate in
the book?

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:36.579
Speaker 5: Yeah, the the secret construct
is the idea that there are a whole bunch of

00:00:36.579 --> 00:00:43.530
interactions or sort of dynamics transactions
in our everyday lives that take the shape

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:49.309
or can take the shape of a scam that feel
like, wait a minute.

00:00:49.309 --> 00:00:55.050
I have a concern here that I'm about to or
I have I'm about to sort of voluntarily enter

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:58.570
into something that's going to leave me holding
the bag.

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:05.369
It's going to leave me with some unfair bad
results, thus making me a sucker.

00:01:05.369 --> 00:01:09.990
And the reason for thinking about this as
a construct is that it can sort of be overlaid

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:15.530
on a variety of situations is not always obvious
that you have like a Ponzi scheme going on.

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:20.260
Speaker 1: What sorts of situations that might
not be obvious, right?

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:23.130
Like outside of actual Ponzi schemes, would
this be relevant?

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:27.590
Speaker 5: Yeah, I think probably some of
the ones that are like most intuitive are

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:32.240
cases where you've gone to buy something and
the price is higher than you thought it should

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:33.689
be.

00:01:33.689 --> 00:01:37.439
So I give an example in the book of my sister
going to buy a Gatorade from a place that

00:01:37.439 --> 00:01:42.399
she really realized when she went in was a
tourist trap and sort of had this moment of

00:01:42.399 --> 00:01:46.210
being like, you know, I'm not one of these
people who pay $6 for Gatorade.

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:50.130
But she was like actually really thirsty and
actually needed to get a Gatorade ASAP.

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:51.860
And she realized, What am I doing?

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:53.399
There's no reason for me to think I'm a sucker
here.

00:01:53.399 --> 00:01:55.770
I just need to buy this drink and move along.

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:59.840
Other situations come up in everyday life
would be like deciding whether I have to believe

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:02.670
somebody who's giving you an excuse for something.

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:03.759
Hmm.

00:02:03.759 --> 00:02:06.479
Deciding whether to let someone cut in front
of you in traffic.

00:02:06.479 --> 00:02:11.300
You know, I think there's a lot of these little
dynamics where people sort of get their hackles

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:16.930
up and think like, what does it make me if
I let you, you know, whenever zoomed down

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:19.265
the breakdown lane and then pop in front of
me on the highway.

00:02:19.265 --> 00:02:24.250
Speaker 1: And one of the arguments that you
make and that seems quite reasonable, is that

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:31.580
this fear of being the sucker impacts personal
choices where if we had maybe a more default

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:39.200
belief that getting suckered wasn't a possibility,
we would behave differently then having this

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:40.750
sort of guard up.

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:44.860
What are the sorts of personal choices that
people would make based on this fear of being

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:45.860
suckered?

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:50.340
Speaker 5: Yeah, maybe I can give you two
examples from two different sort of sides

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:52.430
of the kinds of decisions you might make.

00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:56.599
So I think about this as impacting decisions
that about that are about like getting ahead

00:02:56.599 --> 00:03:01.040
and then impacting decisions that are more
like other regarding so how you sort of do

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:04.709
good rather than sort of how be good in the
world, I guess.

00:03:04.709 --> 00:03:07.500
So one example would be say, personal investing.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:13.311
So there's actually a really nice study that
sort of asks people that ask people in two

00:03:13.311 --> 00:03:17.970
different conditions of a of an experiment,
how much money would you invest in this company

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:19.989
if I gave you the stats of this company?

00:03:19.989 --> 00:03:24.910
And the stats of a company are either, look,
there's an 80% chance that investors break

00:03:24.910 --> 00:03:30.690
even, 15% chance that investors double their
money or 5% chance that they lose it all.

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:35.849
And the 5% chance was this was described a
half a subject as a 5% chance that the founders

00:03:35.849 --> 00:03:37.000
of the company didn't.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:40.150
They had underestimated sorry, they'd overestimated
consumer demand.

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:41.150
They made a mistake.

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:42.730
Speaker 1: Honest mistake.

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:44.010
Speaker 5: Honest mistake.

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:45.010
Exactly.

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:46.010
Yeah.

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:47.010
So you can see where it's going, right?

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:52.349
So the other half of the subjects are told
there's a five that 5% chance that 5% downside

00:03:52.349 --> 00:03:53.349
risk.

00:03:53.349 --> 00:03:54.580
That's the rest of these guys are frauds.

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:56.049
Mm hmm.

00:03:56.049 --> 00:03:58.319
And you know, the numbers there are the same
in.

00:03:58.319 --> 00:03:59.319
Right?

00:03:59.319 --> 00:04:05.140
In both cases, there's a 95% chance of breaking
even or doing better than breaking even.

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:09.120
But people had really different responses
to what to do in those situations.

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:12.280
People were way less willing to invest in
the maybe fraud situation.

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:14.090
You know, only a 5% risk.

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:18.780
Speaker 1: I guess the difference, if you
wanted to argue that there is some logic to

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:26.720
that difference would be, well, if they're
frauds, then we're definitely not getting

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:28.139
our return.

00:04:28.139 --> 00:04:33.720
Whereas if they're wrong, we might just get
a different return or market conditions might

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:34.720
change.

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:37.530
I mean, I get like it's set up in a way where
you're trying to eliminate that, but there

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:39.729
there would be some argument like that, I
guess.

00:04:39.729 --> 00:04:43.970
Speaker 5: No, And I think that that the what
you're describing is actually exactly how

00:04:43.970 --> 00:04:46.570
this works psychologically, like I think you
are.

00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:50.669
You're exactly right to think that the way
it works is that once you invoke the fraud

00:04:50.669 --> 00:04:55.150
possibility, people basically have a whole
bunch of ways that that sort of fraud reasoning

00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:58.310
affects the way they think about the numbers.

00:04:58.310 --> 00:04:59.310
Right.

00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:01.490
I think, well, the fraud number is really
bad.

00:05:01.490 --> 00:05:02.490
That one serious.

00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:04.630
The other number doesn't feel as serious.

00:05:04.630 --> 00:05:05.630
Right.

00:05:05.630 --> 00:05:07.490
Kinds of reasons that you describe and you
can think about.

00:05:07.490 --> 00:05:11.380
The idea is like how how prone are people
to fighting the hypo?

00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:12.380
Right.

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:14.229
They're sort of the what's happening in the.

00:05:14.229 --> 00:05:19.530
Is that you're sort of resisting the math,
which is I think exactly what people are doing

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:23.220
in the real world saying, well, wait, the
math doesn't feel as bad to me.

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:24.220
Right.

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:27.810
And part of the argument is like, that's exactly
that's exactly the point.

00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:32.350
The math feels worse once you invoke the possibility
of a scam.

00:05:32.350 --> 00:05:34.030
Speaker 1: Rather than just a mistake.

00:05:34.030 --> 00:05:35.080
Yeah, that's an interesting one.

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:36.080
What?

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:37.669
What was the other example from a different
area?

00:05:37.669 --> 00:05:42.069
Speaker 5: Yeah, the other example is I think
that people that a lot of people find pretty

00:05:42.069 --> 00:05:48.280
intuitive is that people can get really nervous
or wary about feeling like a sucker when they

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:53.550
start to get in, when they're engaging in
basically like a charitable transaction.

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:59.979
So if you've ever heard of if you've ever
heard people sort of resisting donating money

00:05:59.979 --> 00:06:04.410
or resisting even giving money to somebody
who's asking for money on the street, for

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:06.979
example, they say, yeah, what are they going
to spend it on?

00:06:06.979 --> 00:06:07.979
Right.

00:06:07.979 --> 00:06:09.199
And there's a sort of what what does it make
me?

00:06:09.199 --> 00:06:11.720
If they're going to spend this money on something
that I don't approve of.

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:12.720
Right.

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:18.419
That that's sort of a consistent fear and
there's a whole bunch of sort of animals.

00:06:18.419 --> 00:06:23.030
So there's a whole bunch of pleas, for example,
from food banks.

00:06:23.030 --> 00:06:24.030
Speaker 4: Yes.

00:06:24.030 --> 00:06:29.330
Speaker 5: We're in the phone bank, say we
we really appreciate donations, but please

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:30.330
donate cash.

00:06:30.330 --> 00:06:31.500
Speaker 3: Mm hmm.

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:35.910
Speaker 5: Cash is what we need because we
know what the needs are of the people who

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:36.990
are serving.

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:38.639
We don't pay retail prices, right?

00:06:38.639 --> 00:06:41.009
We pay wholesale prices.

00:06:41.009 --> 00:06:46.020
And we can and we can buy things in bulk.

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:50.169
And a lot of people really resist donating
cash.

00:06:50.169 --> 00:06:55.639
It's a lot easier, I think, to raise to to
to do a charity drive where you're asking

00:06:55.639 --> 00:07:03.120
for in-kind donations, you know, toys, cups,
food, cans of of, you know, canned goods rather

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:04.169
than cash.

00:07:04.169 --> 00:07:07.139
And there's some really interesting studies
that suggest that people basically feel like,

00:07:07.139 --> 00:07:09.700
well, cash is so easy to abuse.

00:07:09.700 --> 00:07:13.800
Cash is going to you know, if I give cash,
I'd be worried or people are going to do with

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:14.800
it.

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:15.930
Whereas if I give a can of beans.

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:19.910
Speaker 1: There's only so many things you
can do with that.

00:07:19.910 --> 00:07:20.910
Speaker 5: Yeah, exactly.

00:07:20.910 --> 00:07:22.949
It's kind of exploitation proof, right?

00:07:22.949 --> 00:07:25.410
Speaker 1: Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:26.639
Speaker 5: Right.

00:07:26.639 --> 00:07:30.270
So, so it's sort of exploitation proof.

00:07:30.270 --> 00:07:37.380
And I know I can see how people can sort of
justify that in their minds, like what's going

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:39.240
on there.

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:44.770
But actually, for any individual, if I was
going to donate or say food to a food drive

00:07:44.770 --> 00:07:47.270
for me to take to get into my car, right.

00:07:47.270 --> 00:07:53.129
And drive to a store and pay retail prices
for a bunch of rice and beans and then to

00:07:53.129 --> 00:07:56.830
bring them to the food bank is actually a
huge waste of time and money.

00:07:56.830 --> 00:08:00.060
And for all I know, they need baby formula
right now, rice and beans.

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:04.710
And so it's actually a way of making this
donation way less valuable and less and way

00:08:04.710 --> 00:08:05.780
more costly for me.

00:08:05.780 --> 00:08:10.180
Speaker 1: When it comes to I mean, we're
sort of kind of getting into some stereotyping

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:14.590
of like, what would the folks who are on the
receiving end be likely to do if they received

00:08:14.590 --> 00:08:16.099
A versus B?

00:08:16.099 --> 00:08:20.350
Can you talk a little more generally about
how stereotyping plays a role in the sucker

00:08:20.350 --> 00:08:21.350
construct?

00:08:21.350 --> 00:08:28.600
Speaker 5: Yeah, I think it plays like a really
under appreciated role or I should say, actually,

00:08:28.600 --> 00:08:34.530
maybe I want to flip the question a little
bit and argue that the ideas we have in the

00:08:34.530 --> 00:08:41.700
fears we have about playing the sucker are
actually constitutive of a lot of intergroup

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:46.260
stereotypes, of a lot of the most pernicious
sort of avenues of bias in those in the culture.

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:53.050
So if you think, for the example I've raised
about about the donations, this is a place

00:08:53.050 --> 00:08:58.470
where I think a lot of people can see that
we live in a society where there are stereotypes,

00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:00.040
for example, about people living in poverty.

00:09:00.040 --> 00:09:01.060
Right, Right.

00:09:01.060 --> 00:09:08.140
That actually what they're doing is taking
your taxpayer dollars and using them to avoid

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:11.420
entering the workforce.

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:18.399
Those kinds of stereotypes are leveraged to
basically reduce solidarity, I would say.

00:09:18.399 --> 00:09:19.399
Right.

00:09:19.399 --> 00:09:24.050
To sort of like to come between people who
want to do the compassionate thing and the

00:09:24.050 --> 00:09:27.459
the compassionate or the sensible or the humane
or however you would describe it, to come

00:09:27.459 --> 00:09:32.750
between them and that instinct and sort of
create rifts between people.

00:09:32.750 --> 00:09:38.529
Speaker 1: And it does seem to follow from
that belief that if you believe that, you

00:09:38.529 --> 00:09:44.540
would say, well, by donating the cans rather
than the money, I am acting logically based

00:09:44.540 --> 00:09:47.130
on this stereotype about the group that would
receive it.

00:09:47.130 --> 00:09:48.860
Speaker 5: Yeah, I think that that's right.

00:09:48.860 --> 00:09:51.710
That's I mean, I think that that's sort of
exactly where people go.

00:09:51.710 --> 00:09:58.709
The more suspicious you are of the people
to whom you are donating goods or money.

00:09:58.709 --> 00:09:59.810
Right.

00:09:59.810 --> 00:10:05.279
The more you go out of your way to only make
a very particular kind of donation.

00:10:05.279 --> 00:10:10.800
I mean, and I would sort of still argue that
that is still sort of at its core an inefficient

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:19.930
donation, in part because there's not actually
I mean, in part because the the the stereotype

00:10:19.930 --> 00:10:24.060
content is we don't have reason to believe
it's true.

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:25.060
Right.

00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:28.360
I think it's you know, it's it's bringing
in a bunch of sort of pernicious classism,

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:29.360
basically.

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:32.500
Speaker 1: When we think about different types
of.

00:10:32.500 --> 00:10:38.500
So, you know, there's like hustles, scams,
Ponzi schemes, business practices to some

00:10:38.500 --> 00:10:39.500
degree.

00:10:39.500 --> 00:10:44.380
You know, we were recently talking about the
FTC now is looking at passing a law that says

00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:48.730
you have to be able to cancel a gym membership
in the same way that you sign up for it.

00:10:48.730 --> 00:10:49.730
Right.

00:10:49.730 --> 00:10:53.639
So it can't be you sign up in 30 seconds online,
but then you've got to go in in person with

00:10:53.639 --> 00:10:55.641
a notarized form in order to cancel it.

00:10:55.641 --> 00:10:57.970
If that's how you have to cancel it, then
you've got to sign up that way, which would

00:10:57.970 --> 00:11:02.660
never happen because the whole point is reduce
friction for the sign up, increase friction

00:11:02.660 --> 00:11:04.399
for for the cancellation.

00:11:04.399 --> 00:11:11.160
That's like a business practice, which you
can argue is a sort of hustle or scam.

00:11:11.160 --> 00:11:18.300
But people's sort of resistance to it is very
different than someone on the street asking

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:19.899
for money.

00:11:19.899 --> 00:11:21.500
What's happening psychologically there?

00:11:21.500 --> 00:11:23.220
Speaker 5: Yeah, it's a great question.

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:26.920
So and I should say that the gym example is
like very close to my heart, in part because

00:11:26.920 --> 00:11:28.500
my day job, I'm teaching contracts.

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:32.230
And so it's a lot of my research is about
consumer contracts and the way people interact

00:11:32.230 --> 00:11:35.350
with this whole world of sort of clicking
to clicking to agree.

00:11:35.350 --> 00:11:36.350
Yeah.

00:11:36.350 --> 00:11:40.790
And one of my deep concerns about the way
that consumer contracts operate in the world

00:11:40.790 --> 00:11:47.290
is that people do feel like they are bound
to these deals and sort of don't perceive

00:11:47.290 --> 00:11:55.360
them as this as exploitation in the way that
you might expect given how hard it is to get

00:11:55.360 --> 00:11:56.380
out of your gym membership.

00:11:56.380 --> 00:11:58.100
Right, Right.

00:11:58.100 --> 00:12:05.040
So so part of the argument that I'm that that
you're picking up on is the argument that

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:12.120
it feels worse to be taken advantage of by
someone who you thought was your subordinate

00:12:12.120 --> 00:12:15.910
or peer than someone who you already assumed
had a bunch of power.

00:12:15.910 --> 00:12:19.839
Do you think that a scam is a power play?

00:12:19.839 --> 00:12:20.839
And I think it is.

00:12:20.839 --> 00:12:25.519
I think I think there's a lot it's about power
in part if you feel like you've been scammed,

00:12:25.519 --> 00:12:29.550
one of the experiences you have is a feeling,
is a feeling for a foolish and humiliated

00:12:29.550 --> 00:12:31.490
or sort of a low standard.

00:12:31.490 --> 00:12:34.930
The lowering of your sense of like what?

00:12:34.930 --> 00:12:40.550
My belonging in this group, my sense of dignity
and belonging, being able to be made to feel

00:12:40.550 --> 00:12:46.850
that way by someone who you already assumed
had power or or you feel you feel less if

00:12:46.850 --> 00:12:52.649
you feel as acutely if it's coming from someone
who you already thought was sort of making

00:12:52.649 --> 00:12:54.380
the world go around.

00:12:54.380 --> 00:13:01.630
Whereas if you think about it as people who
you thought were who you assumed were sort

00:13:01.630 --> 00:13:06.480
of in positions of less power than you, and
it feels like you've been kicked down further

00:13:06.480 --> 00:13:09.260
on the sort of social ladder in ways that
feel really terrible.

00:13:09.260 --> 00:13:10.260
Right.

00:13:10.260 --> 00:13:15.870
And where they feel like destabilizing, like
what is my sense of status and belonging in

00:13:15.870 --> 00:13:18.130
the in this community that's interesting.

00:13:18.130 --> 00:13:24.070
Speaker 1: And that, you know, when I think
about $35 overdraft fees on a checking account,

00:13:24.070 --> 00:13:31.279
which might add up to 500 bucks versus someone
suckered you out of $500 worth of Bitcoin,

00:13:31.279 --> 00:13:37.560
The latter is sort of someone just like you
who figured out a way to get $500 in Bitcoin

00:13:37.560 --> 00:13:41.670
from you versus something that seems more
systematic and sort of like a business oriented

00:13:41.670 --> 00:13:45.850
transaction where you could argue I mean,
I'm not saying that you're told the overdraft

00:13:45.850 --> 00:13:46.850
fees upfront.

00:13:46.850 --> 00:13:50.029
Like I understand that it's not exactly the
same, but the feeling would come away being

00:13:50.029 --> 00:13:51.220
dramatically different.

00:13:51.220 --> 00:13:52.611
Speaker 5: Yeah, I think that that's right.

00:13:52.611 --> 00:13:56.600
I think people feel like there's a there's
a theory in psychology that I like that I

00:13:56.600 --> 00:13:58.829
like a lot called system justification.

00:13:58.829 --> 00:14:05.130
And it's the idea basically that people are
sort of motivated to explain that the system

00:14:05.130 --> 00:14:06.579
works, right?

00:14:06.579 --> 00:14:10.149
So you're motivated to say like, well, you
know, the banks have to make their money somehow.

00:14:10.149 --> 00:14:14.000
And I, I knew, you know, I was alerted beforehand
that his overdraft fee even so, even though

